      "settings": {
        "heading": "Beginner's Guide to Nootropics",
        "subheading": "A conservative, education-first path for understanding labels, evidence limits, contraindications, and when to ask a healthcare professional.",
        "author_name": "",
        "read_time": "10 min read",
        "date_published": "",
        "trust_1": "Education before products",
        "trust_2": "One decision at a time",
        "trust_3": "Labels take precedence",
        "cta_primary_text": "Start with the basics",
        "cta_primary_url": "#what-are-nootropics",
        "cta_secondary_text": "Review safety cues",
        "cta_secondary_url": "#safety"
      }
    },
    "content": {
      "type": "beginners-content",
      "blocks": {
        "h_tldr": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Quick take",
            "level": "h2",
            "anchor_id": "tldr"
          }
        },
        "rt_tldr": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<ul><li><strong>Start with the reason:</strong> define the routine or problem you are trying to understand before looking at products.</li><li><strong>Check the evidence:</strong> human research may be limited, mixed, or unrelated to a product's exact formulation.</li><li><strong>Check the label:</strong> ingredients, warnings, directions, and current availability on the product page take precedence over editorial content.</li><li><strong>Ask first when risk is higher:</strong> pregnancy or nursing, medication use, health conditions, stimulant sensitivity, and competitive-sport rules all warrant professional advice.</li></ul>",
            "sensitivity": "none"
          }
        },
        "pt_quick": {
          "type": "pro_tip",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Keep it simple",
            "content": "<p>Do not begin with a multi-product stack. Review one label and one evidence question at a time so that uncertainty and adverse effects are easier to identify.</p>",
            "icon": "info"
          }
        },
        "h_what": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "What are nootropics?",
            "level": "h2",
            "anchor_id": "what-are-nootropics"
          }
        },
        "rt_what": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>“Nootropic” is a broad marketing and editorial term used for substances associated with cognition, alertness, or stress-related goals. It is not a single regulatory category and does not establish that a product is effective or appropriate for you.</p><p>Evidence differs by ingredient, formulation, population, and outcome. Mechanistic or animal findings should not be treated as proof of a real-world benefit.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "none"
          }
        },
        "h_choose": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "A conservative way to evaluate an option",
            "level": "h2",
            "anchor_id": "how-to-choose"
          }
        },
        "rt_choose": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<ol><li>Write down the specific question you want to answer, such as whether caffeine is affecting sleep.</li><li>Review the full ingredient list, warnings, serving directions, and total stimulant exposure.</li><li>Look for evidence that matches the ingredient, formulation, and outcome rather than relying on testimonials.</li><li>Check medication interactions, contraindications, age restrictions, pregnancy or nursing cautions, and sport or local rules.</li><li>If you proceed, follow the current label and stop if you experience an adverse effect.</li></ol>",
            "sensitivity": "none"
          }
        },
        "pt_choose": {
          "type": "pro_tip",
          "settings": {
            "title": "When to pause",
            "content": "<p>Ask a qualified healthcare professional before use if you take prescription or over-the-counter medication, manage a health condition, are pregnant or nursing, are under 18, or are unsure about an interaction.</p>",
            "icon": "warning"
          }
        },
        "h_quick": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Three educational checkpoints",
            "level": "h2",
            "anchor_id": "quick-start"
          }
        },
        "h_step1": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "1) Foundations first",
            "level": "h3",
            "anchor_id": ""
          }
        },
        "rt_step1": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Sleep opportunity, regular meals, hydration, movement, and distraction controls are lower-risk places to investigate first. Supplements cannot reliably compensate for sleep loss or an untreated medical issue.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "sleep"
          }
        },
        "h_step2": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "2) Read product-level information",
            "level": "h3",
            "anchor_id": ""
          }
        },
        "rt_step2": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>Use the live product page for the current label, ingredient amounts, warnings, directions, and availability. Editorial summaries may become outdated and are not a substitute for the label.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "none"
          }
        },
        "h_step3": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "3) Decide whether professional advice is needed",
            "level": "h3",
            "anchor_id": ""
          }
        },
        "rt_step3": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>A pharmacist or clinician can help assess medication interactions, contraindications, symptoms that need evaluation, and whether a supplement is appropriate. Do not use this guide to self-treat a condition.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "none"
          }
        },
        "pt_step3": {
          "type": "pro_tip",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Label precedence",
            "content": "<p>If this guide conflicts with a current product label or professional advice, follow the label and professional advice.</p>",
            "icon": "info"
          }
        },
        "h_safety": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Safety and interaction cues",
            "level": "h2",
            "anchor_id": "safety"
          }
        },
        "rt_safety": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<ul><li>Avoid assuming “natural” means interaction-free or appropriate for daily use.</li><li>Check total caffeine and other stimulant sources, including coffee, energy drinks, and pre-workout products.</li><li>Ask about interactions with antidepressants, stimulants, sedatives, anticoagulants, blood-pressure medicines, and other medications.</li><li>Stop and seek appropriate care for concerning or persistent symptoms.</li><li>Check current local and sport-governing-body rules; status can change.</li></ul>",
            "sensitivity": "none"
          }
        },
        "rt_safety_caff": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p><strong>Caffeine:</strong> consider anxiety, heart-rate or blood-pressure concerns, sleep timing, other stimulant sources, and individual sensitivity. A combination product does not guarantee the absence of jitters or sleep disruption.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "caffeine"
          }
        },
        "rt_safety_sleep": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p><strong>Sleep:</strong> stimulating ingredients may affect sleep even when taken earlier. Use the label, consider your own sensitivity, and do not use supplements to extend wakefulness at the expense of sleep.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "sleep"
          }
        },
        "rt_safety_gi": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p><strong>Digestive effects:</strong> ingredients can cause nausea or other gastrointestinal effects. Check label directions and stop if effects are significant or persistent.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "gi"
          }
        },
        "rt_safety_anxiety": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p><strong>Anxiety or mood symptoms:</strong> do not use this guide to self-treat. Stimulants and other ingredients may worsen symptoms or interact with medication; seek professional guidance.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "anxiety"
          }
        },
        "h_real": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "How to record observations",
            "level": "h2",
            "anchor_id": "observations"
          }
        },
        "rt_real": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "content": "<p>If you and a qualified professional decide a product is appropriate, record the product and label version, other caffeine or supplements, sleep, and any unwanted effects. A log does not prove causation, but it can make a later professional review more useful.</p>",
            "sensitivity": "none"
          }
        }
      },
